// Formative practice quiz for rendering-lists-with-keys. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design. First draft, not yet reviewed.
$CATEGORY: react/components-and-state/rendering-lists-with-keys

::rendering-lists-with-keys o1 - map and filter::You have an array of products and want a list of only those in stock. What is the usual approach?{
~map first, and return nothing for out-of-stock items, to filter them away#Not quite. map keeps its length, so this leaves undefined entries rather than filtering.
~Write one list item by hand per product#Not quite. That will not follow the data as it changes.
=filter to those in stock, then map each one to JSX#Correct. filter chooses the subset, and map turns each one into an element.
~Use forEach and rely on its return value#Not quite. forEach returns undefined, so nothing would be rendered.
}

::rendering-lists-with-keys o1 - arrow body::A map callback uses curly braces around a JSX element with no return keyword, and nothing renders. Why?{
=A brace body needs an explicit return#Correct. Without return, the callback gives undefined. Parentheses return directly.
~JSX cannot be used inside map#Not quite. Using JSX inside map is the standard pattern.
~The list is missing a key#Not quite. A missing key warns, but items would still render.
~Curly braces make the element a comment#Not quite. They make a function body, not a comment.
}

::rendering-lists-with-keys o2 - index key problem::A list uses the array index as its key, and rows have their own input state. After the first item is removed, what goes wrong?{
~Nothing, because index keys are always unique#Not quite. They are unique but not stable, which is the actual problem.
=Typed values stay in the same positions and now sit beside different items#Correct. Index keys tie state to a position, not to the item.
~All rows lose their state#Not quite. That is what happens with random keys. Index keys keep state in the wrong place.
~React refuses to render the list#Not quite. React renders it without an error.
}

::rendering-lists-with-keys o2 - random key problem::Why is a key from Math.random bad even though it is unique?{
~Keys must always be strings of letters#Not quite. Keys may be numbers or strings. The problem is that it changes.
~Random numbers can match another key#Not quite. A collision is unlikely, and it is not the main problem.
~It sorts the list randomly#Not quite. A key does not change the order of a list.
=It changes on every render, so React recreates rows and loses their state#Correct. A key must be stable as well as unique.
}
